  "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**\n\nI have not found a complete classification of the orbits for general $n$. However, I have rigorously established the following results:\n\n1.  The set of $G$-orbits on $X = G/H \\times G/B$, denoted $G \\backslash X$, is in one-to-one correspondence with the set of double cosets $\\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$, where $B_1$ is a Borel subgroup of $\\SO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})$ and $B_2$ is a Borel subgroup of $\\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$. This provides a combinatorial parameterization of the orbits.\n2.  The number of such orbits is finite.\n3.  For the case $n=1$, I have provided a complete classification. There are exactly three $G$-orbits. I provide explicit orbit representatives, their dimensions, and their closure relations.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\nMy rigorous argument proceeds as follows:\n\n1.  **Geometric Structure:** I first clarify the geometric structure of the space $X = G/H \\times G/B$. The group $G = \\rO_{2n} \\times \\SO_{2n+1}$ is disconnected, with two connected components. A Borel subgroup $B$ of $G$ is a maximal closed connected solvable subgroup, so it is a Borel subgroup of the identity component $G^0 = \\SO_{2n} \\times \\SO_{2n+1}$. The flag variety $\\Flag(G) = G/B$ therefore consists of two connected components, each isomorphic to $G^0/B$. The space $G/H$ is isomorphic to the connected variety $\\SO_{2n+1}$. Consequently, $X$ itself has two connected components. The action of $G^0$ preserves these components, while an element from the non-identity component of $G$ swaps them.\n\n2.  **Orbit Parameterization by Double Cosets:** I establish a canonical bijection between the set of $G$-orbits on $X$ and the double coset space $H \\backslash G / B$. This is a standard reduction for diagonal actions and holds irrespective of the connectedness of the groups involved.\n\n3.  **Reduction of the Double Coset Space:** I simplify the double coset space $H \\backslash G / B$ by constructing an explicit bijection. This leads to the main intermediate result that provides the combinatorial parameterization of the orbits.\n    *   **Lemma:** The map $\\Phi: H \\backslash G / B \\to \\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$ defined by $\\Phi([(g_1, g_2)]) = [\\iota(g_1^{-1})g_2]$ is a well-defined bijection. Here, $[(g_1, g_2)]$ denotes the double coset $H(g_1, g_2)B$ and $[k]$ denotes the double coset $\\iota(B_1)k B_2$.\n\n4.  **Finiteness of Orbits:** I prove that the number of orbits is finite. The parameterization reduces the problem to counting the orbits of the group $S = \\iota(B_1)$ on the flag variety $G_2/B_2$. I show that the pair $(G_2, \\iota(G_1)) = (\\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C}), \\iota(\\rO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})))$ is a symmetric pair. A standard theorem then guarantees that a Borel subgroup of $\\iota(G_1^0)$ (which $S$ is) has a finite number of orbits on $G_2/B_2$.\n\n5.  **Complete Classification for $n=1$:** I provide a full solution for the base case $n=1$.\n    *   The parameterizing space is $\\iota(\\SO_2) \\backslash \\SO_3 / B_2$. The group $S = \\iota(\\SO_2(\\mathbb{C}))$ is a maximal torus in $G_2 = \\SO_3(\\mathbb{C})$.\n    *   The problem reduces to classifying the orbits of a maximal torus on the flag variety $G_2/B_2 \\cong \\mathbb{P}^1$. There are three such orbits: two fixed points and their complement.\n    *   I provide explicit representatives for the three corresponding $G$-orbits on $X$.\n    *   I calculate the dimensions of these orbits. The total space $X$ has dimension 4. There is one open dense orbit of dimension 4, and two closed orbits of dimension 3.\n    *   I describe the closure relations: the two 3-dimensional orbits are closed and form the boundary of the open 4-dimensional orbit.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1. Geometric Structure of the Space $X$\n\nThe group $G = G_1 \\times G_2 = \\rO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C}) \\times \\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$ is a disconnected reductive algebraic group. Its identity component is $G^0 = \\SO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C}) \\times \\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$. The group of connected components $G/G^0 \\cong \\rO_{2n}/\\SO_{2n} \\cong \\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z}$. Let $s_1 \\in \\rO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})$ be an element with $\\det(s_1) = -1$. Then $G = G^0 \\sqcup (s_1, e)G^0$.\n\nA Borel subgroup $B$ of $G$ is a maximal closed connected solvable subgroup. This implies $B$ is a Borel subgroup of $G^0$. Thus, $B = B_1 \\times B_2$, where $B_1$ is a Borel subgroup of $\\SO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})$ and $B_2$ is a Borel subgroup of $\\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$.\n\nThe flag variety $\\Flag(G) = G/B$ consists of two connected components: $G/B = G^0/B \\sqcup (s_1, e)G^0/B$. Each component is isomorphic to the flag variety $G^0/B$.\n\nThe homogeneous space $G/H$ is isomorphic to $G_2 = \\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$ as a variety. This can be seen via the map $\\pi: G/H \\to G_2$ given by $\\pi((g_1, g_2)H) = g_2 \\iota(g_1)^{-1}$, which is a well-defined isomorphism of varieties. Since $G_2$ is connected, $G/H$ is connected.\n\nThe space $X = G/H \\times G/B$ therefore consists of two connected components:\n$X = (G/H \\times G^0/B) \\cup (G/H \\times (s_1, e)G^0/B)$.\nThe action of $G^0$ on $X$ preserves these components, while the action of any element in $G \\setminus G^0$ swaps them. Consequently, every $G$-orbit on $X$ must have a non-empty intersection with both components.\n\n#### 2. Reduction of Orbits to Double Cosets\n\nThe group $G$ acts diagonally on $X$. Any orbit has a representative of the form $(H, gB)$ for some $g \\in G$. To see this, let $(xH, yB)$ be an arbitrary point in $X$. Acting with $x^{-1} \\in G$, we get $x^{-1} \\cdot (xH, yB) = (H, x^{-1}yB)$.\n\nTwo representatives, $(H, gB)$ and $(H, g'B)$, lie in the same $G$-orbit if and only if there exists an element $k \\in G$ such that $k \\cdot (H, gB) = (H, g'B)$. This is equivalent to the conditions $kH = H$ and $kgB = g'B$. The first condition implies $k \\in H$. The second condition then becomes $g'B = kgB$, which means $g'$ is in the double coset $HgB$.\n\nTherefore, the set of $G$-orbits on $X$, denoted $G \\backslash X$, is in one-to-one correspondence with the set of double cosets $H \\backslash G / B$.\n\n#### 3. Bijection with a Simpler Double Coset Space\n\nWe now establish a bijection between $H \\backslash G / B$ and a simpler double coset space that will serve as the combinatorial parameterization of the orbits.\n\n**Lemma:** There is a bijection between the double coset spaces $H \\backslash G / B$ and $\\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$.\n\n**Proof:**\nLet $[(g_1, g_2)]$ denote the double coset $H(g_1, g_2)B$. We define a map $\\Phi: H \\backslash G / B \\to \\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$ by\n$$ \\Phi([(g_1, g_2)]) = [\\iota(g_1^{-1})g_2], $$\nwhere $[k]$ denotes the double coset $\\iota(B_1) k B_2$.\n\n**Well-definedness:** An arbitrary representative of $H(g_1, g_2)B$ has the form $(h_1 g_1 b_1, \\iota(h_1) g_2 b_2)$ for some $h_1 \\in G_1$, $b_1 \\in B_1$, and $b_2 \\in B_2$. Applying the map to this representative gives:\n\\begin{align*} \\Phi([(h_1 g_1 b_1, \\iota(h_1) g_2 b_2)]) &= [\\iota((h_1 g_1 b_1)^{-1}) (\\iota(h_1) g_2 b_2)] \\\\ &= [\\iota(b_1^{-1} g_1^{-1} h_1^{-1}) \\iota(h_1) g_2 b_2] \\\\ &= [\\iota(b_1)^{-1} \\iota(g_1)^{-1} \\iota(h_1)^{-1} \\iota(h_1) g_2 b_2] \\\\ &= [\\iota(b_1)^{-1} \\iota(g_1^{-1}) g_2 b_2] \\end{align*}\nSince $b_1 \\in B_1$, $\\iota(b_1)^{-1} \\in \\iota(B_1)$. Also, $b_2 \\in B_2$. By definition of double cosets, this element represents the same double coset as $\\iota(g_1^{-1}) g_2$. Thus, $\\Phi$ is well-defined.\n\n**Surjectivity:** For any $k \\in G_2$, the double coset $[k] \\in \\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$ is the image of $[(e, k)] \\in H \\backslash G / B$, since $\\Phi([(e,k)]) = [\\iota(e^{-1})k] = [k]$.\n\n**Injectivity:** Suppose $\\Phi([(g_1, g_2)]) = \\Phi([(g'_1, g'_2)])$. This means $[\\iota(g_1^{-1})g_2] = [\\iota(g_1'^{-1})g'_2]$. By definition, there exist $b_1 \\in B_1$ and $b_2 \\in B_2$ such that\n$$ \\iota(g_1'^{-1})g'_2 = \\iota(b_1) \\iota(g_1^{-1}) g_2 b_2. $$\nMultiplying on the left by $\\iota(g'_1)$ gives $g'_2 = \\iota(g'_1) \\iota(b_1) \\iota(g_1^{-1}) g_2 b_2 = \\iota(g'_1 b_1 g_1^{-1}) g_2 b_2$.\nLet $h = g'_1 b_1 g_1^{-1} \\in G_1$. Then $g'_1 = h g_1 b_1^{-1}$.\nThe pair $(g'_1, g'_2)$ can be written as:\n\\begin{align*} (g'_1, g'_2) &= (h g_1 b_1^{-1}, \\iota(h) g_2 b_2) \\\\ &= (h, \\iota(h)) (g_1, g_2) (b_1^{-1}, b_2) \\end{align*}\nSince $(h, \\iota(h)) \\in H$ and $(b_1^{-1}, b_2) \\in B$, this shows that $(g'_1, g'_2)$ is in the double coset $H(g_1, g_2)B$. Therefore, $[(g_1, g_2)] = [(g'_1, g'_2)]$, and $\\Phi$ is injective.\n\nThis establishes that the $G$-orbits on $X$ are parameterized by the double cosets $\\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$.\n\n#### 4. Finiteness of Orbits\n\nThe double coset space $\\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2$ parameterizes the orbits of the group $S = \\iota(B_1)$ acting by left multiplication on the flag variety $G_2/B_2$. We show the number of orbits is finite.\n\nLet $G_2 = \\SO_{2n+1}(\\mathbb{C})$. Let $J = \\mathrm{diag}(I_{2n}, -1)$. The map $\\sigma(g) = J g J^{-1}$ is an involutive automorphism of $G_2$. The subgroup of fixed points is $L = G_2^\\sigma = \\{g \\in G_2 \\mid \\sigma(g) = g\\}$. A matrix $g \\in G_2$ commutes with $J$ if and only if it is of the block-diagonal form $\\mathrm{diag}(A, d)$. For $g$ to be in $\\SO_{2n+1}$, $A$ must be in $\\rO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})$ and $\\det(A)d=1$, which implies $d=\\det(A)$. Thus, $L = \\iota(G_1)$.\nThe pair $(G_2, L)$ is a symmetric pair. The identity component of $L$ is $L^0 = \\iota(\\SO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C}))$. The group $B_1$ is a Borel subgroup of $\\SO_{2n}(\\mathbb{C})$, so its image $S = \\iota(B_1)$ is a Borel subgroup of $L^0$.\n\nA fundamental result from the theory of spherical varieties states that for a symmetric pair $(G,L)$, a Borel subgroup of $L^0$ has a finite number of orbits on the flag variety $G/B$. Applying this theorem with $G=G_2$, $L=\\iota(G_1)$, and their respective Borel subgroups $B_2$ and $S=\\iota(B_1)$, we conclude that the number of $S$-orbits on $G_2/B_2$ is finite.\n\n#### 5. Complete Classification for the case $n=1$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have $G_1 = \\rO_2(\\mathbb{C})$ and $G_2 = \\SO_3(\\mathbb{C})$. The Borel subgroup $B_1$ is a subgroup of $G_1^0 = \\SO_2(\\mathbb{C})$. The group $\\SO_2(\\mathbb{C}) \\cong \\mathbb{C}^*$ is a one-dimensional torus, so it is its own unique Borel subgroup. Thus, $B_1 = \\SO_2(\\mathbb{C})$.\n\nThe set of orbits is parameterized by $\\iota(B_1) \\backslash G_2 / B_2 = \\iota(\\SO_2) \\backslash \\SO_3 / B_2$.\nThe group $S = \\iota(\\SO_2(\\mathbb{C}))$ is a maximal torus of $G_2 = \\SO_3(\\mathbb{C})$. The problem reduces to classifying the orbits of a maximal torus $S$ on the flag variety $G_2/B_2$.\n\nThe group $G_2 = \\SO_3(\\mathbb{C})$ is isomorphic to $\\mathrm{PGL}_2(\\mathbb{C})$. Under this isomorphism, a maximal torus $S$ corresponds to the subgroup of diagonal matrices, and a Borel subgroup $B_2$ corresponds to the subgroup of upper-triangular matrices. The flag variety $G_2/B_2$ is isomorphic to the projective line $\\mathbb{P}^1$. The action of $S$ on $\\mathbb{P}^1$ has exactly three orbits: two fixed points and their complement, which is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{C}^*$.\n\nThus, for $n=1$, there are exactly **three** $G$-orbits on $X$.\n\n**Orbit Representatives:**\nThe three orbits of $S$ on $G_2/B_2$ correspond to three double cosets in $S \\backslash G_2 / B_2$. Let $W_2 = \\{e, w_0\\}$ be the Weyl group of $G_2$. The fixed points are $eB_2/B_2$ and $w_0B_2/B_2$. The open orbit is represented by $uB_2/B_2$ for an element $u$ in the unipotent radical of the opposite Borel subgroup.\nThe three $G$-orbits on $X$ can be represented by points $(H, kB)$ where $k$ is a representative for the corresponding double coset in $H \\backslash G / B$. We can choose $k=(e, k_2)$ where $k_2 \\in \\{e, w_0, u\\}$.\n1.  $\\mathcal{O}_e = G \\cdot (H, B) = \\{ (gH, gB) \\mid g \\in G \\}$.\n2.  $\\mathcal{O}_{w_0} = G \\cdot (H, (e, w_0)B) = \\{ (gH, g(e, w_0)B) \\mid g \\in G \\}$.\n3.  $\\mathcal{O}_u = G \\cdot (H, (e, u)B) = \\{ (gH, g(e, u)B) \\mid g \\in G \\}$.\n\n**Dimensions and Closure Relations:**\nThe dimension of an orbit $\\mathcal{O}_k = G \\cdot (H, kB)$ is $\\dim G - \\dim(\\text{Stab}_G(H, kB))$. The stabilizer is $H \\cap k B k^{-1}$.\nFor $n=1$, $\\dim G = \\dim \\rO_2 + \\dim \\SO_3 = 1+3=4$.\nThe space $X \\cong G_2 \\times (G^0/B \\sqcup (s_1,e)G^0/B)$ has dimension $\\dim G_2 + \\dim(G^0/B) = 3 + (\\dim G^0 - \\dim B) = 3 + (4-3) = 4$.\n\nThe stabilizer calculation gives $\\dim(H \\cap (e,k_2)B(e,k_2)^{-1}) = \\dim(S \\cap k_2 B_2 k_2^{-1})$.\n*   For $k_2=e$: $\\dim(S \\cap B_2) = \\dim S = 1$. So $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_e = 4-1=3$.\n*   For $k_2=w_0$: $\\dim(S \\cap w_0 B_2 w_0^{-1}) = \\dim(S \\cap B_2^-) = 1$. So $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{w_0} = 4-1=3$.\n*   For $k_2=u$: $\\dim(S \\cap u B_2 u^{-1}) = 0$. So $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_u = 4-0=4$.\n\nThe orbit $\\mathcal{O}_u$ is the unique open dense orbit. The orbits $\\mathcal{O}_e$ and $\\mathcal{O}_{w_0}$ are of lower dimension. The closure relations on the orbits of $S$ on $G_2/B_2$ lift to the $G$-orbits on $X$. The two fixed points are in the closure of the open orbit.\n*   $\\mathcal{O}_u$ is open and dense in $X$.\n*   $\\mathcal{O}_e$ and $\\mathcal{O}_{w_0}$ are closed orbits.\n*   The closure of the open orbit is the entire space: $\\overline{\\mathcal{O}_u} = X = \\mathcal{O}_u \\cup \\mathcal{O}_e \\cup \\mathcal{O}_{w_0}$. The boundary of $\\mathcal{O}_u$ is the disjoint union of the two closed orbits: $\\partial \\mathcal{O}_u = \\mathcal{O}_e \\cup \\mathcal{O}_{w_0}$.",
